Corven liked the guild house best when it was empty.

By afternoon the long chamber belonged to him. The men were down in the Pit and the yard was hammering. He lit the magic lanterns one at a time down the length of the hall. Each one woke with an orange hum. He counted them as he went. Fourteen. Last winter there had been nine. He had bought five more from a lamp wizard in Moonfall Hollow and paid them off by spring, and every man who worked under them now worked an extra hour in the dark months without complaint. Fourteen lanterns. An extra hour. Two hundred men. He had done that arithmetic many times. It never stopped being pleasant.

At the last lantern, he paused and looked at his reflection in the brass. Mustache neat. Hair black. He licked his thumb and smoothed a gray strand at his temple back under the black. The dye from Phineas had cost him more than the lanterns. Worth it. Men followed a black-haired man into the dark. They hesitated behind a gray one.

He sat at the desk at the far end, near the hearth, and opened the ledger.

The Collegium was coming. A boy had brought the little one's note at noon, while half the village was still up on the cemetery hill watching the Bone Man turn its dead to dust. Corven had not gone up the hill. Someone had to keep the mine running.

The note was written in a hand so tight it looked like insect tracks. Regarding the matter of sanction. Corven read it twice, then burned it. Then he spent the hour before they came with the ledgers, moving numbers.

Not lying. Corven never lied in a ledger. He arranged.

--

They came at the fourth bell. Corven heard the horse before he saw it. Or rather, he heard nothing. The coal yard went quiet the way it did when the pit bell rang. Hammers stopped. Then started again, slower.

He did not get up. He stayed seated, the ledger open, quill in hand, and let them come down the length of the hall to him under his fourteen lanterns.

The little one came first. Pellin. He carried his clay tablet against his chest like a breastplate, and he was already talking.

"Guild master, good afternoon, though I use the word good with some reservation, as your coal yard has a drainage trench that runs directly across the only path from the square and I have now ruined my second pair of hose since arriving, which I will be noting, not as a grievance, you understand, merely as an observation of infrastructure, which is, after all, the Collegium's concern where magic is used in its construction, and I am given to understand that your trench was cut by a mining wizard, which raises the question of whether it was registered --"

"It was," said Corven. "Page forty. Year before last."

Pellin stopped. He blinked his little mole eyes.

Behind him, the man in white walked down the hall.

He did not hurry. He did not look at the lanterns, or at the men working in the yard through the windows, or at the ledgers stacked along the wall. He looked only at Corven. The robe was white. Not white the way laundered linen was white, with a little gray at the seams. White the way snow was white, the morning after, before anyone had stepped in it. In a coal town. In a guild house where the dust had been ground into the floorboards for two years.

Corven decided he hated that robe.

He stood then, but slowly, and held out his hand. Let the man see the black in his knuckles. Let him see what work looked like.

The man in white looked at the hand. Then he took it. His grip was dry and cool and exactly as firm as Corven's, not one hair more. He let go first.

"Guild master."

"Sit," said Corven. "Please." He nodded at the stool by the hearth. The low one. He had swapped them before they came. His own chair had a back and arms and sat a full hand higher.

The man in white looked at the stool. He did not sit. He stood by the fire with his hands folded in front of him and looked down at Corven, who was now seated again, and Corven realized he had lost that one before it began.

Fine. There would be others.

"Ale?" said Corven. He poured two cups from the jug on the hearth without waiting for an answer. He slid one across the corner of the desk. It sat there. The man in white did not look at it.

Pellin had found the other stool and perched on it, tablet on his knees, stylus already wet. "The matter of sanction," he said, "arises from three distinct violations of the Tenets of Magic, which I will enumerate, and I would ask you to hold your responses until the enumeration is complete, as I find that interruptions tend to produce incomplete records, and incomplete records are the root of very nearly every --"

"Matthis isn't a guild wizard," said Corven. "Never was. He's not on the rolls. You can check."

"I have checked," said Pellin, wounded. "That is violation the second. An unregistered practitioner operating within a guild's jurisdiction, under a guild's charter, with the guild's evident knowledge --"

"He's the Death Guild. Not the Miner's Guild. Different charter. Town-owned as of three days ago." Corven sipped his ale. "Vicar drew it up. Very neat hand."

Pellin's stylus hovered. His mouth opened and closed. He turned to the man in white, the way a dog turns to its master when a squirrel has gone up a tree.

The man in white said nothing. He was looking at the second cup of ale. At the ring of wet it had left on the desk.

"Then the third violation," said Pellin, gathering himself, "concerns the mine itself, and the use of magic in its excavation, which has, according to the Guild's own figures, increased by a factor of nearly three in the last two quarters, without a corresponding increase in reported effects, which is, frankly, statistically --"

"Impossible," said the man in white.

The first word he had said since Guild master. Corven turned it over. Impossible. Not unusual. Not irregular. Impossible.

"My men are careful," said Corven.

"No," said the man in white. "They are not."

Corven smiled from his cheeks. He set down his cup. He leaned back in his tall chair, steepled his fingers under his chin, and let the silence sit long enough to make Pellin shift on his stool.

"The Collegium's welcome to anything above the grass," Corven said. "My yard. My hall. My ledgers. Pellin there's already read half of them. But below the grass, sir, the mine is the Guild's. By charter. Royal seal, and older than your Collegium's, if I'm not mistaken." He was not mistaken. He had checked that too, before they came. "You want to go down there, you'll need my say-so. And you'll need a lamp. And a man who knows the way. And all three of those things belong to me."

The man in white's jaw moved.

It was nothing. A muscle, a single small flex beneath the skin, there and gone. His gloved fingers tightened on each other, very slightly, the way a man's might if he were holding back from touching a sore tooth. His gray eyes went flat for half a breath. Then the calm closed back over it, smooth as water over a dropped stone.

But Corven had seen it.

Twenty years of haggling over coal weights, wages, and timber. Twenty years of watching men's faces when they thought they had him. He knew that flicker. It was the face of a man who was not used to being told no and had just been told no by someone he considered beneath him.

Corven picked up his ale again so his face had something to do. Inside, he was grinning like a boy.

The man in white opened his mouth.

The door at the far end of the hall banged open.

--

Bartholomew stood in the doorway, black from boots to eyebrows. His chest heaved. He had run. Corven had never seen Bartholomew run anywhere. Bartholomew strolled into collapses. He made jokes over corpses.

He was not making a joke now. His face beneath the coal dust was the color of ash.

"Corven." He gulped air. "Deep Seven."

Corven stood. His ale sloshed.

"What's happened? Is it the timber? I told Hadley to double the --"

"Nobody's hurt." Bartholomew shook his head. Then he shook it again, as though trying to shake something loose. His hand was in his pocket, gripping something there. "Nobody's hurt. That's not -- It's the magic, Corven."

"What about it?"

Bartholomew looked past him. At the man in white. Something passed over his face that Corven could not read.

"It's working right."

Pellin made a small, impatient sound. "Magic always works, Master Wizard, that is precisely the difficulty, it works and then it produces an effect, and the effect is what must be --"

"No." Bartholomew's voice cracked. "Nothing's coming back. Godric's cast six times. Six. Nothing comes back."

The hall was very quiet. One of the fourteen lanterns buzzed.

The man in white was already walking.

--

Corven led. It was his mine. He took the lantern from its hook at the shaft mouth, the good one with the wizard stone that burned bright and steady, and he did not offer the man in white another.

Down the metal ladder. Along Old Bed, crouching under the low ceiling. Pellin complained about the ceiling, the ladder, the smell, the dust, the absence of a proper inventory of the mine's support timbers, and then about his hose again, until somewhere in Left Fork he went quiet and stayed quiet.

They passed the flesh-colored bulb in the wall. It pulsed. Corven did not look at it. He knew what it had cost: three days of digging around it, two broken picks, and one miner who refused to work that stretch ever again and had to be moved to the yard at reduced wages. He kept that in a column. It was easier there.

They passed the place where the cave-in had been. The smooth stone. Corven slowed there, as he always did. Twenty-two names. He had written every one of them in the ledger himself, in his best hand. He paid the widows' stipends from the Guild account. Eleven years, he had promised them, and he had done the sums before he promised. The Guild could carry it. But the old seams were thinning, and the Guild could only carry it if the Pit kept producing what it produced now.

That was why Deep Seven. He had timbered it twice as thick as it needed. He did not rush a seam. Rushed seams buried men, and buried men cost more than slow coal.

Clean magic was cheap magic. Cheap magic was more coal. More coal was eleven years.

He walked faster.

Deep Seven was new. Two months old. The tunnel was raw, the timbers still pale and sweet-smelling. It sloped down more steeply than any other shaft in the Pit. The air turned cool and damp and smelled of wet stone. The sweat from the guild house went cold on Corven's neck. Behind him, the man in white walked without a sound.

He heard the miners before he saw them. Or rather, he didn't. A dozen men in a chamber should have made noise. Coughing, cursing, the scrape of boots. There was nothing. Only the drip of water and, somewhere, a man whispering the same few words over and over.

The tunnel opened into the chamber. Lamps everywhere, but every one of them had been set on the floor and turned toward the walls. A dozen miners stood in a loose half-circle with their backs to the coal face. Not one of them was looking at it. Hadley stood with his eyes shut, lips moving. Young Tam from the yard crouched against the far wall with his head between his knees, and the smell told Corven he had already been sick. Godric stood a little apart from the rest, a pick in one fist, staring at the floor.

When they saw Corven, the men shifted. Their eyes went to him, to the man in white, to the tunnel behind them. Back to Corven. Not one of them looked behind them.

Corven lifted his lantern.

--

At first Corven thought it was a man buried in the stone.

It stood half out of the coal face, the way a fossil stands half out of shale. Arms straight out from the shoulders, level and perfect, like a man stretched on a beam. Legs together. Head up. The miners had dug it free to the waist. Below that, it vanished into black rock.

It was gray. Not the gray of stone. Smoother. There were no chisel marks. No pores. No grain. Where the head should have had a face, the surface simply curved, the way an egg curves, and the lamplight slid across it without catching.

It was not dusty.

Every surface in Deep Seven was black with coal dust. The timbers. The floor. The miners. Corven's own sleeves. The shape had been buried in coal for longer than Coalridge had existed, longer than the Blackveins had had a name, and not a single grain clung to it.

Corven found he had lowered the lantern. He made himself raise it again. His eyes kept sliding off the thing, the way they slid off the sun. Every part of him wanted to look at the floor, the timbers, the men, anything else. It was not ugly. That was the worst of it. There was nothing in it to be afraid of. And still every hair on his arms stood up, the way it did when a seam was about to go.

"Show them," Corven said. His voice came out strange.

Godric did not move at first. Then he stepped forward without lifting his eyes from the floor. He held out his hand toward a chunk of rock at the base of the coal face, a boulder the size of a cart wheel, well to one side of the thing. His lips moved. The boulder split cleanly in two and fell open like a loaf.

Corven waited.

Nothing grew. No crystal. No flowers. No bleeding wall, no warm fleshy bulb, no gravel pouring from a timber. The two halves of the boulder lay on the floor. That was all.

That was all.

One of the miners made a sound, low in his throat. Hadley's whispering got faster.

Godric turned to them. His grizzled face was wet. "Six times," he said. "Seven now."

Bartholomew had stopped at the mouth of the tunnel. He had not come any further. He took the stone out of his pocket. Corven had seen him fiddle with it for months. It was small and gray. It did not catch the lamplight either. Bartholomew looked at it for a long moment. Then he put it back in his pocket and kept his hand over it.

Corven thought of eleven years. He thought of a thing in his mine that no one could explain, and what that would cost him if it stayed a mystery.

"Bartholomew," he said. "Break it."

Bartholomew's head snapped up.

"Corven." His voice was hoarse. "I don't think we should --"

Corven said nothing. He only looked at him.

In two years, Bartholomew had never once argued with an order. He grumbled. He mocked. He made his little jokes about wizards and their limits. And then he did the job, every time, with a shrug, as though the work were beneath him and he did it anyway as a favor.

He did not shrug now. He stood at the mouth of the tunnel with his hand over his pocket, and he did not move.

The men watched him. Corven could feel them watching. Some of them, he thought, were hoping Bartholomew would refuse. Some of them were hoping he would do it and get it over with. All of them wanted to be anywhere else.

Bartholomew hesitated.

Corven had seen him walk into a collapse with a joke on his lips. He had seen him haul dead friends out of the dark. He had never seen him hesitate.

Then Bartholomew stepped forward. One step. Another. He stopped an arm's length from the thing and did not look at it. He looked at Corven the whole time. He raised his hand.

He did not speak the words aloud. Corven saw his lips move. Saw his arm tense. Saw sweat start on his temple.

Nothing happened. Not to the figure. Not to the coal around it. Not even the air stirred. It was as though Bartholomew had shouted into a well with no bottom.

Bartholomew lowered his hand. He backed away until he reached the tunnel mouth again, and he stayed there.

The drip of water stopped. The timbers stopped creaking. Hadley's whispering stopped. The lamp flames stood perfectly still, not a flicker. A dozen miners stood holding their breath. Pellin's mouth hung open, and no sound came out of it. Corven could not hear his own heart. He was not sure it was beating.

The figure stood with its arms out. It did not move. It had never moved. It would never move.

And yet Corven could not shake the feeling that it was waiting.

--

He was aware, slowly, of the shovels.

Every man in the chamber was holding one. He did not know when they had picked them up. Some held them loosely at their sides. Some held them across their bodies like staves. Tam had gotten to his feet against the wall and was gripping his so hard his knuckles had gone white. None of them had moved toward the coal face. All of them were looking at the man in white.

So was Corven.

The man in white stood at the edge of the lamplight. His face was as calm as it had been in the guild house. His hands were folded as they had been in the guild house. But a bead of sweat had formed at his hairline. It hung there. It trembled. It ran down his temple and into the collar of the snow-white robe.

He looked at the men. At their shovels. At the thing in the wall. He let out a breath that might, in another man, have been a sigh.

"Bury it," he said.

The miners moved before the words had finished. Shovels bit into the rubble, and coal and rock flew against the gray surface, and slid off, and they shoveled faster, harder, every man at once, as though they had been waiting their whole lives to be allowed.

Corven would have joined them if he had held a shovel. Pellin was scratching nonsense into his tablet, his eyes anywhere but on the men and what they buried. The white gloves clenched, as though they itched to join the burial. Corven nodded to himself. Every soul in the chamber felt the need to hide from the stone figure.

Pellin had not said a word. Corven had not thought the little man capable of it.

Corven stood with his lantern and watched his men obey someone else. It stung. He noted the sting, the way he noted everything, and set it aside for later.

Bit by bit, the arms, the shoulders, the smooth egg of a head disappeared under black.

The drip of water returned. A timber creaked. Somewhere far up the shaft, a pick rang on stone.

--

They walked out in silence. Even Pellin.

At the base of the metal ladder, the man in white stopped to let Corven go up first with the lantern. As Corven turned, the lamplight swung low, across the floor, across the hem of the white robe.

There was a spot on it.

Small. No bigger than a thumbprint. Black.

Coal dust.

Corven looked at it for exactly as long as it took to set his foot on the first rung. Then he climbed and did not look back.

--

The guild house was empty when he returned. Someone had let the hearth burn down. The two cups of ale still sat on the corner of his desk. One full.

He drank it.

Then he sat in his tall chair, under his fourteen lanterns, and opened the ledger to a fresh page. He dipped his quill. In his best hand, at the top of the page, he wrote: Deep Seven. Sealed by order of the Collegium.

He blotted it. He turned the page.

On the next page, in a smaller hand, he wrote: Deep Seven. Clean.

He looked at the word for a long time. He thought of the spot on the white hem. He thought of the bead of sweat. He thought of a man who had never once been told no.

He would not touch it. Not this year. Perhaps not ever. He was not a fool. But a man who knew where clean magic was buried was a man who would never again have to take no from a white robe.

Corven smiled from his cheeks.

He closed the ledger.
